do $seed$
declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 7, $j$2.3 The Gospel Goes to the Gentiles$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Acts 10 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Acts/10"}, {"title": "Acts 11 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Acts/11"}, {"title": "Acts 13 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Acts/13"}, {"title": "Acts 15 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Acts/15"}], "body": "Read Acts 10, Acts 11, Acts 13 and Acts 15 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nA Gentile is a person who is not Jewish. At first nearly all believers were Jews, but God showed Peter in a vision that the good news was for everyone. Peter went to the house of Cornelius, a Roman centurion in Caesarea, and as he preached, the Holy Ghost fell on those who heard. In Antioch many Gentiles believed, and the disciples were first called Christians there. The church at Antioch sent out Barnabas and Saul on a missionary journey. Saul was also called Paul. When some said Gentile believers had to keep the law of Moses, the apostles and elders met in Jerusalem and agreed that Gentiles are saved by the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ.\n\nKey words: Gentile, Cornelius, Antioch, missionary, council.\n\nYour task: (1) Explain what a Gentile is. (2) Tell what Peter learned from the vision and the house of Cornelius (Acts 10:34 and 35). (3) Tell where believers were first called Christians.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Last time we saw Saul become a follower of Jesus. Today we see something even bigger. The good news starts to cross the line between Jews and Gentiles. A Gentile is a person who is not Jewish. Up to this point almost every believer was Jewish, and many of them did not think the Gentiles could be included.\n\nGod had to change Peter's mind. Peter was staying in Joppa when he saw a vision of a sheet let down from heaven with all kinds of animals, and a voice told him to rise, kill and eat. Peter said he had never eaten anything common or unclean. The voice said, What God hath cleansed, that call not thou common. While Peter was thinking about it, men came from a Roman centurion named Cornelius, a devout man who prayed to God and gave to the poor. An angel had told Cornelius to send for Peter.\n\nPeter went to his house in Caesarea and said in Acts 10:34 and 35, Of a truth I perceive that God is no respecter of persons: But in every nation he that feareth him, and worketh righteousness, is accepted with him. While Peter was still speaking, the Holy Ghost fell on all those who heard, and they were baptized. The door had opened to the Gentiles.\n\nIn the city of Antioch many Gentiles believed. Acts 11:26 says, the disciples were called Christians first in Antioch. The church there prayed and fasted, and the Holy Ghost said to set apart Barnabas and Saul for a special work. They traveled to Cyprus and to cities in Asia Minor and preached to Jews and Gentiles. Saul now went by his Roman name, Paul.\n\nSome men from Judaea taught that the Gentile believers had to be circumcised and keep the law of Moses. The apostles and elders gathered in Jerusalem to settle the question, and Peter reminded them that we believe that through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ we shall be saved. The council wrote a letter to encourage the Gentile believers, and everyone rejoiced.\n\nYour task is to explain what a Gentile is, tell what Peter learned at the house of Cornelius, and tell where believers were first called Christians. Then take the four question check.\n\nThe grace of God is for every nation. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$2.3 The Gospel Goes to the Gentiles$j$);
end
$seed$;
